<GuidePage title='Client/Server Boundaries - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 5 · Part A: Maximize the Known</div>
  <h1>Client/Server Boundaries</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    The question is never "does this component need <code>"use client"</code>?" It's "how far down
    can I push the boundary?" This chapter is about what the directive actually does, what genuinely
    requires the client, and the patterns for isolating interactivity into the smallest possible
    leaves.
  </p>

  <h2 id='what-it-means'>5.1 What <code>"use client"</code> actually means</h2>
  <p>
    <code>"use client"</code> is not a per-component setting, and it isn't "this component is interactive."
    It is a <strong>module-graph marker</strong>: it declares the point where the server hands the
    tree to the browser. The directive goes at the top of a file, and from that file onward,
    <em>every module it imports, and everything those import</em>, becomes client code: bundled,
    shipped, parsed, and hydrated. <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    That's why one careless directive is never one component's problem. A
    <code>"use client"</code> at the top of a page doesn't make a page interactive: it drags the page's
    entire import graph across the boundary. Components that render pure markup, utilities that format
    dates, the icon set, the table internals: all of it ships and hydrates, whether or not it ever does
    anything a server component couldn't have done for free.
  </p>

