  <p>
    Three mechanical facts complete the picture: <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Client components still render on the server once.</strong> "Client" doesn't mean "skips
      SSR": their initial HTML is server-rendered, then hydrated in the browser. What you lose at the
      boundary isn't the first paint; it's <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>
      data access, zero-bundle rendering, and everything else in Part A.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Props crossing the boundary must be serializable.</strong> The server sends them over the
      wire: plain objects, arrays, strings, numbers: yes; functions, class instances, DB handles: no.
      (Server Actions and JSX are the special, allowed exceptions.) If you're fighting serialization errors,
      the boundary is probably in the wrong place.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The dependency arrow points one way.</strong> A client module can't import a server component;
      importing it would just drag it across and make it client code. Server content gets <em
        >into</em
      > client subtrees only by being passed through, already rendered (<a href='#children-pattern'
        >§5.4</a
      >).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id='what-requires'>5.2 What genuinely requires the client, and what doesn't</h2>
  <p>The honest list of things that <em>cannot</em> run on the server is short:</p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Needs the client</th>
        <th>Does not need the client</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>State that changes from user input:</strong>
          <code>useState</code>,
          <code>useReducer</code>
        </td>
        <td
          ><strong>Fetching and awaiting data:</strong> that's what server components are <em
            >for</em
          ></td
        >
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Event handlers:</strong>
          <code>onClick</code>, <code>onChange</code>,
          <code>onSubmit</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <strong>Rendering data:</strong> lists, tables, conditionals, formatting, markup of any complexity
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Effects and lifecycle:</strong>
          <code>useEffect</code> (and it had better not be fetching;
          <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>)
        </td>
        <td>
          <strong>Reading route input:</strong>
          <code>params</code> and <code>searchParams</code> arrive as page props (<a
            href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a
          >)
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Browser APIs:</strong>
          <code>localStorage</code>, observers, media queries,
          <code>window</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <strong>Composition:</strong> laying out children, choosing variants, mapping data to components
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Interactivity hooks:</strong>
          <code>useRouter</code>, <code>useOptimistic</code>,
          <code>useTransition</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <strong>Authorization decisions:</strong> cookie/session reads belong on the server, with
          <code>redirect()</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Third-party components</strong> built on any of the above</td>
        <td>Anything whose <strong>output is the same HTML for the same inputs</strong></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Read the right column again: it's most of every page. That's the empirical reason "use client
    everywhere" is backwards: the default assumption should be that a component is
    server-renderable, with the burden of proof on the exception. When a component needs
    <em>one</em> item from the left column, the answer is not to convert the component; it's to <strong
      >evict that one item into a leaf</strong
    > and keep everything else on the server.
  </p>

  <h2 id='push-down'>5.3 Pushing the boundary down</h2>
  <p>
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> The method, applied every time something needs interactivity:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Name the interaction atoms.</strong> What does the user actually
      <em>do</em> here? Type in a search box. Click an export button. Open a row menu. Those atoms, not
      the sections containing them, are your client components.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Extract each atom into its own leaf file.</strong> A <code>SearchInput</code>, an <code
        >ExportButton</code
      >, a <code>RowActionsMenu</code>: each with
      <code>"use client"</code> at the top, each owning exactly its interaction and nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep the structure server-side.</strong> The section, the card, the toolbar row, the table
      markup: all of it stays server; it renders data, it doesn't handle input.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where interactivity must <em>wrap</em> content, pass the content through.</strong>
      A collapsible panel, a dialog, a hover card: the shell is client, but its children arrive server-rendered
      (<a href='#children-pattern'>§5.4</a>).
    </li>
  </ol>

