  <p>
    This is also why <strong>no <code>"use client"</code> in the app directory</strong> is the house recommendation.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The app directory is the scaffold: pages, layouts, structure, core content (<a
      href='03-mental-model.html'>§3</a
    >). If a file in <code>app/</code> carries the directive, the scaffold itself has crossed the boundary,
    and everything slotted into it goes along. Client code lives in leaf components, imported <em
      >into</em
    > the scaffold. Legitimate exceptions exist (<code>error.tsx</code>
    is required to be a client component; a page that is genuinely one interactive surface, like a canvas
    editor), but each one should be an explicit decision, not a reflex to make a hook error go away.
  </p>

  <h2 id='children-pattern'>5.4 Getting server content inside client shells</h2>
  <p>
    The pattern that makes leaf boundaries workable:
    <strong
      >a client component can't <em>import</em> server components, but it can
      <em>receive</em> them.</strong
    >
    <code>children</code> (and any JSX-valued prop) is rendered on the server first; the client shell
    receives the finished output and slots it in. The interactive wrapper ships to the browser; its contents
    don't. <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Wrapper converts its contents'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">CollapsibleSection.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-com">/* Importing it drags it across the boundary:
   ActivityFeed is client code now, fetch and all. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { ActivityFeed } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"./ActivityFeed"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">CollapsibleSection</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [open, setOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;section&gt;
      &lt;button <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(!open)} /&gt;
      {open &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ActivityFeed</span> /&gt;}
    &lt;/section&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ Wrapper receives server-rendered children'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">CollapsibleSection.tsx: client shell, knows nothing</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">CollapsibleSection</span>({ children }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [open, setOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;section&gt;
      &lt;button <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(!open)} /&gt;
      {open &amp;&amp; children}
    &lt;/section&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">page.tsx: server composes through the shell</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">CollapsibleSection</span>&gt;
  <span class="tok-com">{/* ActivityFeed stays a server component; it
      fetches, renders, and ships 0 KB of JS; it
      passes through the shell as HTML. */}</span>
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ActivityFeed</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">CollapsibleSection</span>&gt;</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>
  <p>
    One behavior of this composition is worth being explicit about:
    <strong
      ><code>ActivityFeed</code> fetches its data when the page loads, whether or not the section is open.</strong
    > The server renders every child passed through the shell during the page's render; <code
      >&#123;open &amp;&amp; children&#125;</code
    > only controls whether the already-rendered HTML is <em>displayed</em>. Collapsed by default
    changes nothing: the fetch already happened on the server. Usually that is exactly what you want
    (opening the section is instant, no spinner), and the cost is one possibly-unused query. But if
    the requirement is that the fetch must <em>not run until the user opens the section</em>,
    because the query is expensive or rarely wanted, then by definition the data is being requested
    in response to an interaction, and interaction-driven fetching is client-side fetching: a client
    component that queries on open (SWR,
    <a href='15-dedupe-caching.html#swr-depth'>§15.6</a>) behind the same shell. That is a
    legitimate trade, but make it deliberately: you are exchanging an instant open for a loading
    state the container now has to absorb.
  </p>
  <p>
    This slot pattern is the workhorse for every interactive shell: dialogs, sheets, tabs,
    accordions, tooltips, hover cards. The shell owns open/closed and nothing else; what's
    <em>inside</em> keeps all its server privileges. It's also the answer to context providers: a <code
      >ThemeProvider</code
    > or <code>QueryClientProvider</code> is a client component, but rendered as a thin wrapper around
    <code>&#123;children&#125;</code> near the root, it converts nothing below it: the children pass through,
    still server components.
  </p>

  <h2 id='third-party'>5.5 Third-party components and boundary hygiene</h2>
  <p>The rest of the category, the housekeeping that keeps boundaries where you put them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong
        >Wrap third-party interactive components in your own
        <code>"use client"</code> file.</strong
      >
      <Chip k='next' />
      <Chip k='critical' /> External UI libraries use state and events throughout, but many don't declare
      <code>"use client"</code> (it's a bundler convention, not a JS standard; older packages predate
      it). Importing them from a server component fails or silently misbehaves. One-line wrapper file:
      <code>"use client"; export &#123; Carousel &#125; from "acme-carousel";</code>. Now it has a
      declared boundary, and your import graph documents exactly where the client zone starts.
      Bonus: the wrapper is your seam for swapping the library later (<a
        href='16-component-breakdown.html'>§16</a
      >). Worked through with examples in
      <a href='#internalize-third-party'>the subsection below</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Poison-pill your server-only modules.</strong>
      <Chip k='react' />
      <Chip k='very' />
      <code>import "server-only"</code> at the top of the data layer (<a
        href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a
      >) turns "someone imported <code>data/programs.ts</code> from a client component" into a build error
      instead of a leaked API key. <code>client-only</code> exists for the reverse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mind the environment variables.</strong> Only <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_*</code> vars exist in client
      bundles, and all of them are shipped to every visitor. If a boundary move suddenly requires making
      a variable public, that's the boundary telling you it's in the wrong place. <Chip k='next' />
      <Chip k='critical' />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Don't reach for client hooks to read route input.</strong>
      <code>params</code> and <code>searchParams</code> arrive as server page props;
      <code>useParams</code>/<code>useSearchParams</code> in a client page is usually the
      <em>reason</em> the page went client in the first place (<a
        href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a
      >). Same for cookies and headers: <code>next/headers</code>, on the server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Authorization is a server decision.</strong> A guard component that fetches "am I allowed?"
      after hydration paints the page first and asks permission later. The cookie is on the server; check
      it in the layout or page and
      <code>redirect()</code>/<code>notFound()</code> before anything renders.
      <Chip k='next' />
      <Chip k='critical' />
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id='library-leaves'>
    Design-system components: ship the structure and the leaves separately
  </h3>
  <p>
    Everything this chapter says about applications applies with extra force to the <strong>monorepo UI library</strong>, because a library component's boundary decision is made
    once and inherited by every consumer. The rule:
    <strong
      >a library component with interactive parts exposes its non-interactive structure and its
      interactive leaves as separate components</strong
    >, so the consuming application gets to place the boundary, not the library.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    The canonical mistake is the all-or-nothing component configured through data. A sidebar that
    takes <code>items=&#123;[&#123; icon, label, onClick &#125;]&#125;</code> is one fused component:
    it must carry <code>"use client"</code> (it wires handlers), and because
    <strong>functions cannot cross the server-client boundary as props</strong>, the page that
    renders it is forced client too, just to be able to define those
    <code>onClick</code>s. One prop signature has converted two trees.
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Fused: configured with data + functions'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">app layout (forced client)</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Sidebar</span>
  <span class="tok-prop">items</span>={[
    {
      <span class="tok-prop">icon</span>: &lt;<span class="tok-fn">HomeIcon</span> /&gt;,
      <span class="tok-prop">label</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Home"</span>,
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>: () =&gt; router.<span class="tok-fn">push</span>(<span class="tok-str">"/home"</span>),
    },
  ]}
/&gt;</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          Here <code>onClick</code> is a function, and functions cannot cross the server-client boundary,
          so this layout must be a client component just to describe its sidebar. The library made the
          boundary decision for every consumer.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ Composed: structure + leaf items'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">app layout (stays server)</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Sidebar</span>&gt;
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SidebarLinkItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/home"</span> <span class="tok-prop">icon</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">HomeIcon</span> /&gt;}&gt;
    Home
  &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">SidebarLinkItem</span>&gt;
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SidebarLinkItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/reports"</span> <span class="tok-prop">icon</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">BarIcon</span> /&gt;}&gt;
    Reports
  &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">SidebarLinkItem</span>&gt;

  <span class="tok-com">{/* The one interactive item is an app-level
      client leaf that owns its handler (§5.7). */}</span>
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SignOutItem</span> /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Sidebar</span>&gt;</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          No directive anywhere in this file: the container and link items are server-safe because
          navigation is an href, not a click handler. Only the sign-out leaf ships JavaScript, and
          it owns its own click.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    Inside the library, that means three small files instead of one big one. Note which of them
    carries the directive, and which do not need it at all:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">packages/ui/sidebar/Sidebar.tsx: structure, no directive</span><span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Sidebar</span>({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;nav <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"sidebar"</span>&gt;{children}&lt;/nav&gt;;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Pure structure: renders anywhere, server included, and ships nothing.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">packages/ui/sidebar/SidebarLinkItem.tsx: navigation, no directive</span><span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SidebarLinkItem</span>({ href, icon, children }: <span class="tok-fn">SidebarLinkItemProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Link</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>={href} <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"sidebar-item"</span>&gt;
      {icon}
      {children}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Link</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Navigation is a link. An href is serializable, so this needs no directive either, and
      prefetching comes free with the Link (<a href='04-ssr.html#prefetching'>§4.5</a>).
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">packages/ui/sidebar/SidebarButtonItem.tsx: the interactive leaf, own file</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SidebarButtonItem</span>({ icon, onClick, children }: <span class="tok-fn">SidebarButtonItemProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;button <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"sidebar-item"</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={onClick}&gt;
      {icon}
      {children}
    &lt;/button&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The one part that genuinely needs an event handler, isolated in its own file so that importing
      the container never drags it across the boundary.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    One nuance closes the loop: a server layout still cannot pass <code>onClick</code> to
    <code>SidebarButtonItem</code> (functions don't cross the boundary), and that is by design. Button-style
    items get consumed by small app-level client leaves, like the
    <code>SignOutItem</code> above, that render <code>SidebarButtonItem</code> and own the handler themselves,
    exactly the self-contained-leaf pattern of
    <a href='#self-contained'>§5.7</a>. The library provides the pieces; the application decides
    where the client zone starts.
  </p>

  <h3 id='internalize-third-party'>
    Internalize third-party components: one wrapper file, one leaf
  </h3>
  <p>
    The first bullet above deserves its worked example, because the failure mode is subtle: it is
    not just that an undeclared third-party component breaks under a server import. It is that
    importing it <em>directly</em> from your own components makes
    <strong>every composite that touches it</strong> a client component, layout, labels, help text and
    all, and every component composing <em>those</em> composites after it.
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Imported directly: the composite goes client'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramPicker.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> Select <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react-select"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramPicker</span>({ programs }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Label</span>&gt;Program&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Label</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Select</span> <span class="tok-prop">options</span>={<span class="tok-fn">toOptions</span>(programs)} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">HelpText</span>&gt;Pick one to continue.&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">HelpText</span>&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          Because <code>react-select</code> is interactive, this entire composite goes client: the layout,
          the label, the help text. Every component that embeds <code>ProgramPicker</code> inherits the
          same fate.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ Internalized: the third-party part is the leaf'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">ui/Select.tsx, then ProgramPicker.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export</span> { <span class="tok-kw">default as</span> Select } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react-select"</span>;</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          The third-party component is internalized once, behind one internal client file: this wrapper
          is the leaf, and nothing else needs the directive.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramPicker.tsx: no directive</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { Select } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/ui/Select"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramPicker</span>({ programs }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Label</span>&gt;Program&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Label</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Select</span> <span class="tok-prop">options</span>={<span class="tok-fn">toOptions</span>(programs)} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">HelpText</span>&gt;Pick one to continue.&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">HelpText</span>&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          The composite stays a server component; only the internalized Select ships JavaScript. The
          wrapper is also the single seam for theming or replacing the library later.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <h2 id='searchparams-boundary'>
    5.6 Client components that read the URL get their own Suspense boundary
  </h2>
  <p>
    One boundary case is mechanical enough to deserve its own pattern: it sits exactly at the
    intersection of this chapter and
    <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a> (the URL as state). A client component that calls
    <code>useSearchParams()</code>
    <strong>must render inside a <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> boundary.</strong>
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    Why: search params are <em>per-request data</em>. While Next.js prerenders the static shell of a
    page, there is no request, so the value genuinely does not exist yet, and a client component
    that insists on reading it from the browser has to be able to suspend until it does. What the
    boundary buys, concretely:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The shell stays static.</strong> The rest of the page prerenders and streams normally; only
      the reader of the search params waits for request time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The bailout is scoped.</strong> Without a local boundary, the client-side-rendering bailout
      climbs to the nearest ancestor boundary, often deopting the <em>entire page</em> to client rendering
      to accommodate one filter input. With one, the "dynamic hole" is exactly the size of the leaf.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The build passes under PPR.</strong> With <code>cacheComponents</code>, a request-time
      read outside a Suspense boundary isn't a deopt; it's an error. The boundary is how you declare
      "this part is dynamic, on purpose."
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because forgetting the wrapper is easy and the failure is confusing, make the boundary part of
    the component itself; consumers should never need to remember it:
  </p>
  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">SearchInput.tsx: the boundary is part of the component's public shape</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SearchInput</span>(props: <span class="tok-fn">SearchInputProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">SearchInputSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SearchInputInner</span> {...props} /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
  );
}

<span class="tok-com">// Only the inner component reads the URL, so only it ever suspends.</span>
<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SearchInputInner</span>(props: <span class="tok-fn">SearchInputProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> params = <span class="tok-fn">useSearchParams</span>();
  <span class="tok-com">// …</span>
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The exported component bakes its own <code>Suspense</code> boundary in, so no call site can forget
      it. The fallback mirrors the input's geometry (same reserved space, <a
        href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
      >), so nothing shifts when it resolves.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>
  <p>
    Two clarifications to keep the pattern honest. First, this applies to the
    <em>client</em> read: a server page reading <code>searchParams</code> from its props doesn't need
    this; the page is simply dynamic at that point, which is
    <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a>'s territory. Reach for the client read only in
    leaves that <em>write</em> the URL and need the live value interactively (filter inputs, sort toggles).
    Second, <code>useParams</code> and <code>usePathname</code> don't require this: route params and pathname
    are knowable from the route structure; it's specifically the query string that is per-request. See
    it in action in
    <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a>'s worked example, where the boundary also
    carries an explanatory comment.
  </p>

  <h2 id='self-contained'>5.7 Encapsulation in practice: the self-contained button</h2>
  <p>
    Everything this chapter has argued comes to a point in one concrete pattern. The purest
    expression of "push the boundary down" is an interactive leaf that
    <strong>owns its entire interaction</strong>: the click, the pending state, the call to the
    server, and any UI the interaction spawns.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> Nothing above it participates, which is precisely what lets everything above
    it stay a server component.
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ The page owns the interaction'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/page.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-com">/* One button's mechanics hoisted the whole
   page into the client. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramPage</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPublishing, setPublishing] =
    <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>={isPublishing}
        <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-fn">setPublishing</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>);
          <span class="tok-kw">await</span> api.<span class="tok-fn">publish</span>(programId);
          <span class="tok-fn">setPublishing</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);
          <span class="tok-fn">refetchProgram</span>();
        }}
      &gt;
        Publish
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ The leaf owns the interaction'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/page.tsx</span><span class="tok-com">/* Server component: no "use client",
   no handlers, no loading flags. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramPage</span>(
  { params }
) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { id } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(id);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">program</span>={program}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    The button itself is small, boring, and completely portable. It calls a server action, and <code
      >useTransition</code
    > gives it a pending flag for the duration: its loading state, owned locally, exactly as the loading
    doctrine demands (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#no-own-loading'>§11.3</a>; a <em
      >pending interaction</em
    > indicator on the control is the legitimate kind of local loading state; it reserves no content space
    and replaces nothing):
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/PublishButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useTransition } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* Yes, a client component can import a server action. The "use server"
   module doesn't pull server code into the bundle: the client receives a
   typed reference, and calling it becomes a network request the framework
   manages. This is the one import that crosses the boundary by design. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { publishProgram } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/actions/programs"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* The entire interaction lives here: the click, the pending state, and the
   call to the server. Nothing above this component knows any of it exists. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span>({ programId }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>={isPending}
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-com">/* Actions return results rather than throwing across the network:
             respond to the failure right where the interaction lives. */</span>
          <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { error } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(programId);
          <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (error) toast.<span class="tok-fn">error</span>(error);
        })
      }
    &gt;
      Publish
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Pause on that import, because it's the part people don't believe: a
    <em>client</em> component imported a <em>server</em> function and called it in a click handler. That
    is exactly what server actions are for. The
    <code>"use server"</code> directive turns the module's exports into references the client can hold;
    importing one adds no server code to the bundle, and invoking it makes a typed, framework-managed
    request to the server (the imports that are
    <em>forbidden</em> across the boundary are server components and
    <code>server-only</code> modules, per <a href='#what-it-means'>§5.1</a>). So the boundary can
    sit at the button and the mutation still runs where it belongs: no API route, no fetch wrapper,
    no client-side secret handling.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same rule covers everything an interaction spawns.
    <strong>A dialog opened by a button lives inside the button component that opens it</strong>, as
    do popovers, dropdown menus, drawers, and every other floating element.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> The open/closed state is interaction state; it belongs to the leaf. The moment
    the dialog's state lives in the page, the page is client, and every sibling of that dialog pays for
    one confirmation prompt.
  </p>
  <p>
    Encapsulating the dialog also hands you a bundle win for free: since the dialog is invisible
    until its button is clicked, its JavaScript has no business in the first-render bundle. Load it
    with <code>next/dynamic</code> and render it conditionally: the chunk downloads on first open, and
    until then the page carries only the button (<a href='07-first-render-js.html'>§7</a>'s subject,
    met here in its most common form):
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/DeleteProgramButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* The dialog's JavaScript isn't needed until the button is clicked, so it
   doesn't belong in the first-render bundle (§7). next/dynamic splits it
   into its own chunk, fetched on demand. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> ConfirmDialog = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt;
  <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"@/components/dialogs/ConfirmDialog"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.ConfirmDialog)
);

<span class="tok-com">/* The dialog is an implementation detail of the button that opens it. It
   mounts here; the page never knows a dialog exists. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">DeleteProgramButton</span>({ programId }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [open, setOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span> <span class="tok-prop">variant</span>=<span class="tok-str">"danger"</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>)}&gt;Delete&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;

      <span class="tok-com">{/* Mounted only once opened, so the chunk loads on first click. */}</span>
      {open &amp;&amp; (
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ConfirmDialog</span>
          <span class="tok-prop">open</span>
          <span class="tok-prop">onOpenChange</span>={setOpen}
          <span class="tok-prop">title</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Delete this program?"</span>
          <span class="tok-prop">onConfirm</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">deleteProgram</span>(programId)}
        /&gt;
      )}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Look at the wire count. The parent hands each of these components an <code>id</code>: identity,
    the one prop that's always legitimate (<a href='14-minimize-the-wires.html'>§14</a>). That is
    the entire contract. No <code>onPublish</code>, no
    <code>isPublishing</code>, no <code>dialogOpen</code>, no refetch callback. Which raises the
    obvious question: when the action finishes and the page's data is stale,
    <em>who tells the parent?</em>
  </p>

  <h2 id='refresh'>5.8 Refreshing the parent without wiring it to the child</h2>
  <p>
    Two honest answers exist, and the difference between them is most of this guide's thesis in
    miniature.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Approach 1: a refetch context.</strong> Wrap the region in a provider that owns the query
    and exposes its re-execute function; the button calls
    <code>useRefetch()</code> and stays ignorant of who actually refires:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-warn'
      is:raw><span class="filename">The context approach: workable, but note what it costs</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> { refetch } = <span class="tok-fn">useRefetch</span>();

<span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
    <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>={isPending}
    <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
      <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
        <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(programId);
        <span class="tok-fn">refetch</span>();
      })
    }
  &gt;
    Publish
  &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
);</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Inside the button: no prop threading, and no knowledge of who the parent is.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    This is genuinely better than drilling an <code>onSuccess</code> callback through three layers: the
    intermediate components stay clean. But look at what it presumes:
    <em>someone above is fetching in client land</em>, or there would be no refetch to offer. The
    provider and the query pull the boundary back up the tree, client-rendering a region
    to solve a problem the platform already solves. Acceptable inside a genuinely client island (see
    the SWR variant below); the wrong default for a page.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Approach 2: the server is the source of truth.</strong>
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> The mutation already runs on the server; let the server decide what's stale.
    The action invalidates the data it changed, Next.js re-renders the server components that read that
    data, and a fresh RSC payload (<a href='04-ssr.html#routing'>§4.5</a>) streams back and merges
    in place. Nobody calls anybody:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">actions/programs.ts</span><span class="tok-dir">"use server"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { revalidateTag } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/cache"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(id: <span class="tok-kw">string</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">try</span> {
    <span class="tok-kw">await</span> db.program.<span class="tok-fn">update</span>({ <span class="tok-prop">where</span>: { id }, <span class="tok-prop">data</span>: { <span class="tok-prop">status</span>: <span class="tok-str">"published"</span> } });
  } <span class="tok-kw">catch</span> (e) {
    <span class="tok-com">/* Returned, not thrown: the client handler decides how to surface it
       (the toast in §5.7). */</span>
    <span class="tok-kw">return</span> { <span class="tok-prop">error</span>: <span class="tok-str">"Could not publish the program. Please try again."</span> };
  }

  <span class="tok-com">/* The server is the source of truth. Invalidating the tag re-renders every
     server component whose data carried it; the parent page updates without
     ever being told to. revalidatePath() is the coarser equivalent, and
     router.refresh() covers mutations that didn't go through an action. */</span>
  <span class="tok-fn">revalidateTag</span>(<span class="tok-str">`program-${id}`</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> {};
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

