  <p>
    <strong>Reads follow the exact same principle</strong>: the URL plays the role the cache tag
    played. A search input doesn't call an <code>onSearch</code> prop; it writes its query param and lets
    the route re-render:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/table/SearchInput.tsx (inner; see §5.6 for the baked-in boundary)</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SearchInputInner</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> router = <span class="tok-fn">useRouter</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> searchParams = <span class="tok-fn">useSearchParams</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SearchField</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={(value: <span class="tok-kw">string</span>) =&gt; {
        <span class="tok-kw">const</span> params = <span class="tok-kw">new</span> <span class="tok-fn">URLSearchParams</span>(searchParams);
        <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (value) params.<span class="tok-fn">set</span>(<span class="tok-str">"q"</span>, value);
        <span class="tok-kw">else</span> params.<span class="tok-fn">delete</span>(<span class="tok-str">"q"</span>);

        <span class="tok-com">/* A client-side transition to the same route: the server page
           re-renders with the new searchParams and streams fresh rows into
           the table. The input doesn't know a table exists; the table
           doesn't know the input exists. The URL is the only wire. */</span>
        router.<span class="tok-fn">replace</span>(<span class="tok-str">`?${params.toString()}`</span>);
      }}
    /&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    This is the pattern <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a>'s worked example
    builds out in full (with the transition-based stale indicator), and
    <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a> makes the general case for the URL as state. Notice
    what the two halves share:
    <strong
      >the leaf writes to shared infrastructure (the cache, the URL) and the parent re-renders
      because its <em>inputs</em> changed</strong
    >, never because a child called it. That is also §14's core rule, seen from the boundary side.
  </p>

  <h3 id='swr-refresh'>When the parent is legitimately client: the key is the contract</h3>
  <p>
    Sometimes the parent has earned its <code>"use client"</code>, and the canonical case is UI that <strong
      >doesn't exist until the user does something</strong
    >. A drawer that slides open off a row click, a details popover, a preview panel: none of these
    are on the page when the server renders it, so their content can never ride the first response.
    When they mount, after the interaction, they fetch client-side, and
    <code>useSWR</code> is the right tool. The encapsulation pattern survives intact; only the shared
    infrastructure changes. SWR's cache is keyed, and
    <strong>the key replaces the callback</strong>:
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/ProgramDrawer.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> useSWR <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"swr"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramDrawer</span>({ programId, onClose }: <span class="tok-fn">ProgramDrawerProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data: program, isLoading } = <span class="tok-fn">useSWR</span>(
    [<span class="tok-str">"program"</span>, programId],
    () =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">fetchProgram</span>(programId)
  );

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Drawer</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={onClose}&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* Loading ⊥ data ⊥ empty, handled per §11.6; elided here. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramDetails</span> <span class="tok-prop">program</span>={program} <span class="tok-prop">isLoading</span>={isLoading} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Drawer</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      This component is legitimately client: it opens after an interaction, so the server's first
      render never contains it, and its data is necessarily fetched after mount. It fetches by key,
      and passes the button an id (identity, nothing else), exactly as the server version did.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/PublishButton.tsx (SWR flavor)</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { mutate } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"swr"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span>({ programId }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>={isPending}
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(programId);
          <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">mutate</span>([<span class="tok-str">"program"</span>, programId]);
        })
      }
    &gt;
      Publish
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The button revalidates the key when its mutation lands. Every subscriber of that key refires
      on its own; the button holds no reference to the parent, receives no refetch callback, and
      works under any parent that uses the same key.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Server tag, URL param, SWR key: three spellings of one idea. The leaf and the parent agree on a <em
      >name</em
    > for the data, and communicate by invalidating the name. The refetch mechanics live in the infrastructure
    (<a href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a>), not in a callback prop, so the boundary stays at the
    leaf no matter which spelling the page uses.
  </p>

  <h2 id='god-components'>5.9 The God component: one boundary to lose everything</h2>
  <p>
    Now invert every rule in this chapter at once, because the Tracker codebase does. Put a
    <code>"use client"</code> component (or its more respectable-looking cousins, a provider or a <code
      >useXManager()</code
    > hook) at the top of a tree, and have it do three jobs:
    <strong>fetch the data, own the UI state, and dispense the callbacks</strong>, flowing all of it
    down through props and context:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-bad'
      is:raw><span class="filename">The shape to fear: three jobs in one hook</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">useProgramsManager</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [programs, setPrograms] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Program</span>[]&gt;();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isLoading, setIsLoading] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">true</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [filters, setFilters] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Filters</span>&gt;(DEFAULT_FILTERS);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [selectedId, setSelectedId] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>&lt;<span class="tok-kw">string</span> | <span class="tok-kw">null</span>&gt;(<span class="tok-kw">null</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isSaving, setIsSaving] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);

  <span class="tok-fn">useEffect</span>(() =&gt; {
    <span class="tok-com">/* Fetch #1 of the tree: nothing below renders content until this lands. */</span>
    <span class="tok-fn">fetchPrograms</span>(filters).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((data) =&gt; {
      <span class="tok-fn">setPrograms</span>(data);
      <span class="tok-fn">setIsLoading</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);
    });
  }, [filters]);

  <span class="tok-kw">async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">saveProgram</span>(input: <span class="tok-fn">ProgramInput</span>) { <span class="tok-com">/* … */</span> }
  <span class="tok-kw">async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">deleteProgram</span>(id: <span class="tok-kw">string</span>) { <span class="tok-com">/* … */</span> }

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> {
    programs, isLoading,
    filters, setFilters,
    selectedId, setSelectedId,
    isSaving, saveProgram, deleteProgram,
  };
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Three jobs in one hook: it fetches, it owns everyone's UI state, and it defines everyone's
      callbacks. Every consumer below becomes a client component wired to this one object's shape.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    That's the hook spelling. The same disease wears a component costume just as often: a page or
    container that fetches everything in sight, holds state it never reads, and threads all of it
    down through layers that are nothing but couriers:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-bad'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/page.tsx: the component spelling</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramsPage</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Fetches #1, #2, and #3, none of which the page itself renders. It is
     collecting cargo on behalf of components two and three levels down. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data: programs, isLoading } = <span class="tok-fn">useSWR</span>(<span class="tok-str">"/api/programs"</span>, fetcher);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data: me } = <span class="tok-fn">useSWR</span>(<span class="tok-str">"/api/me"</span>, fetcher);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data: orgs } = <span class="tok-fn">useSWR</span>(<span class="tok-str">"/api/organizations"</span>, fetcher);

  <span class="tok-com">/* State the page holds only so it can hand it to descendants. The page
     never filters anything and never selects anything. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [filters, setFilters] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Filters</span>&gt;(DEFAULT_FILTERS);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [selectedId, setSelectedId] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>&lt;<span class="tok-kw">string</span> | <span class="tok-kw">null</span>&gt;(<span class="tok-kw">null</span>);

  <span class="tok-com">/* Every layer below is a courier. SectionLayout doesn't use `user` or
     `orgs`; the toolbar doesn't select; the table hands `selectedId` on
     down to Row, which hands it to Cell. Boxes carrying boxes. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SectionLayout</span> <span class="tok-prop">user</span>={me} <span class="tok-prop">orgs</span>={orgs}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramsToolbar</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">user</span>={me}
        <span class="tok-prop">filters</span>={filters}
        <span class="tok-prop">onFiltersChange</span>={setFilters}
      /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramsTable</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">rows</span>={programs}
        <span class="tok-prop">isLoading</span>={isLoading}
        <span class="tok-prop">filters</span>={filters}
        <span class="tok-prop">selectedId</span>={selectedId}
        <span class="tok-prop">onSelect</span>={setSelectedId}
        <span class="tok-prop">onDelete</span>={(id) =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">deleteProgram</span>(id)}
      /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">SectionLayout</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Read the page's own render: it uses <em>none</em> of what it gathered. Every fetch, every piece of
    state, every callback exists on behalf of someone else: the page is a warehouse, and <code
      >SectionLayout</code
    >, <code>ProgramsToolbar</code>, and
    <code>ProgramsTable</code> are its delivery routes (the coupling-layer autopsy of
    <a href='14-minimize-the-wires.html#cost'>§14.1</a>, wearing its boundary costume). And the
    threading doesn't stop where the snippet does: <code>selectedId</code> rides through
    <code>ProgramsTable</code> into <code>Row</code> into <code>Cell</code>, coupling four
    signatures to one piece of state that exactly one of them reads.
  </p>

  <p>
    Call this what it is: a <strong>God component</strong>. It is hazardous in a way ordinary bad
    code isn't, because the damage isn't local. The
    <code>"use client"</code> sits at the top, so the module graph (<a href='#what-it-means'>§5.1</a
    >) drags <em>the entire subtree</em> across the boundary: every section, table, row, and cell below
    it is client-rendered now, whether it has an event handler or not. And that means the App Router's
    server-first machinery isn't just unused down there. It is
    <strong>literally unavailable</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No server components below it.</strong> An <code>async</code> component can't render under
      a client parent (only pass <em>through</em> one, via slots,
      <a href='#children-pattern'>§5.4</a>, which this shape never uses because it imports
      everything it renders).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No first-paint content, no cached pages.</strong> The data loads after hydration, so the
      first response is a shell: nothing to stream (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#streaming'
        >§11.2</a
      >), nothing for the route cache to keep (<a href='04-ssr.html#page-cache'>§4.4</a>). Every
      visitor re-pays every fetch, forever.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The timing problems return.</strong> Content depends on request ordering again: waterfalls
      (<a href='13-blocking-requests.html#client'>§13.4</a>), nested spinners (<a
        href='04-ssr.html#simplicity'>§4.2</a
      >), layout shift as each answer lands (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Maximum wiring.</strong> Every consumer is coupled to the manager's return shape; every
      new feature threads more props and callbacks through components that don't use them. This half of
      the autopsy, the coupling half, is
      <a href='14-minimize-the-wires.html#callbacks'>§14.4</a>'s subject.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refactor gravity.</strong> Children written against the manager's context can't be lifted
      out or rendered on the server without rewriting them, so the cheapest place to add feature N+1 is
      inside the God component, which is how it got this big last time.
    </li>
  </ul>

