  <p>
    The fix is <em>not</em> to split the God component into three smaller gods. It's to invert the direction
    of everything it does: data flows down as
    <strong>rendered HTML</strong> instead of props (<a href='#push-down'>§5.3</a>), interactions
    live in <strong>self-contained leaves</strong> instead of dispensed callbacks (<a
      href='#self-contained'>§5.7</a
    >), and staleness flows through
    <strong>the cache and the URL</strong> instead of refetch functions (<a href='#refresh'>§5.8</a
    >). Each child stops being a puppet of the manager and becomes a component again.
  </p>

  <h2 id='server-actions'>5.10 Server actions and the HTTP middleman</h2>
  <p>
    <a href='#self-contained'>§5.7</a>'s button called a server action and moved on. It is worth
    stopping on what that server action actually is, because it is the single biggest lever for
    pulling mutations back across the boundary, and the reason the 73 pass-through proxy handlers
    the audit found (<a href='18-applied-to-tracker.html#finding-1'>§18</a>) are unnecessary.
  </p>
  <p>
    A server action is a <strong>typed remote procedure call</strong>. When you mark an async
    function <code>"use server"</code>, the bundler splits it out of the client graph, leaves a
    reference in its place, and the framework generates an endpoint for it. Calling it from the
    client serializes the arguments, sends them to that endpoint, runs the function on the server,
    and serializes the result back. You write an ordinary function call; the framework does the
    transport. Because the function's signature is the contract, the types flow from the component
    to the server and back with no schema to hand-maintain and no client to keep in sync. The API
    surface (the directive,
    <code>useActionState</code>, <code>useFormStatus</code>, <code>&lt;Form&gt;</code>) is
    catalogued in <a href='19-new-primitives.html#actions'>§19.2</a> and the form-side flow gets
    <a href='#forms'>§5.11</a>; what matters here is the architecture.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason it matters beyond ergonomics is what you <strong>lose</strong> when you put an HTTP layer
    you own between the UI and the data.
  </p>

