    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      The same mutation, two architectures. A server action is one typed boundary from the client
      straight to your data, so distinct failures stay distinct and the types hold end to end.
      Funnel it through proxy API routes and each HTTP hop flattens your errors into status codes
      and adds its own failure modes: network, timeout, serialization. Fewer middlemen, fewer things
      to handle.
    </Fragment>
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    When a server action reaches your data directly, through Prisma or a service SDK, a failure is a
    typed value you handle precisely: the record does not exist, the user is not allowed, a
    constraint failed, each one distinct and catchable at the call site. Route that same mutation
    through a Next.js API route that proxies to another HTTP service (the Tracker pattern from
    <a href='18-applied-to-tracker.html#finding-1'>§18</a>) and every one of those distinct failures
    is flattened into a status code on the way out and reconstituted on the way back. You also
    inherit a second set of failure modes that have nothing to do with your domain: network errors,
    timeouts, retries, serialization mismatches. Each middleman is one more place the types are lost
    and one more surface of errors the caller has to account for.
  </p>

  <h3 id='actions-orm'>The direct version: the ORM inside the action</h3>
  <p>
    Server actions are built for this. The ordinary Prisma pattern (and it works the same with any
    ORM or query builder) is to use the client <em>directly inside the action</em>: no API between
    the interaction and the data at all. That buys latency (one process, no serialization hops),
    flexibility (select exactly the fields this action needs, nothing more), and above all a <strong
      >private error surface</strong
    >: the failure cases are enumerated and handled here, in context, for this one interaction,
    instead of being designed to satisfy a hundred other callers of a shared endpoint.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/actions.ts - Prisma, no API in between</span><span class="tok-dir">"use server"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { prisma } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/lib/db"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">archiveProgram</span>(id: number) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> prisma.program.<span class="tok-fn">findUnique</span>({
    <span class="tok-prop">where</span>: { id },
    <span class="tok-com">// Just the fields THIS action needs to decide.</span>
    <span class="tok-prop">select</span>: { id: <span class="tok-kw">true</span>, status: <span class="tok-kw">true</span> },
  });

  <span class="tok-com">/* null is a domain answer, not a status code: the row
     does not exist. Name the failure for this interaction
     and return it in the type. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (!program) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> { error: <span class="tok-str">"This program no longer exists."</span> };
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (program.status === <span class="tok-str">"archived"</span>) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> {};

  <span class="tok-kw">await</span> prisma.program.<span class="tok-fn">update</span>({
    <span class="tok-prop">where</span>: { id },
    <span class="tok-prop">data</span>: { status: <span class="tok-str">"archived"</span> },
  });
  <span class="tok-fn">revalidateTag</span>(<span class="tok-str">`program-${id}`</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> {};
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The return type is inferred, so <code>&#123; error &#125;</code> flows to the button in
      <a href='#self-contained'>§5.7</a> with no schema to maintain. A <code>null</code> from
      <code>findUnique</code> can mean exactly one thing, and the action decides what this interaction
      should do about it.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='actions-central-api'>The centralized API, called directly: still fine</h3>
  <p>
    None of this outlaws a dedicated backend. If a centralized API layer exists (other consumers,
    another team, another runtime), calling it <em>directly from the action</em>
    is a perfectly good architecture; the action becomes the adapter that narrows generic HTTP semantics
    into this interaction's domain answers. What you give up, and should give up knowingly, is precision.
    Compare the error surface:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/actions.ts - centralized API, called directly</span><span class="tok-dir">"use server"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">archiveProgram</span>(id: number) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> res = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">fetch</span>(<span class="tok-str">`${API_URL}/programs/${id}/archive`</span>, {
    <span class="tok-prop">method</span>: <span class="tok-str">"POST"</span>,
    <span class="tok-prop">headers</span>: <span class="tok-fn">authHeaders</span>(),
  });

  <span class="tok-com">/* One status code, several possible truths. 404: the
     program is gone? the route moved in the last deploy?
     the resource was renamed? The action has to guess,
     and the response body's type is whatever we assert. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (res.status === <span class="tok-str">404</span>) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> { error: <span class="tok-str">"This program no longer exists."</span> };
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (!res.ok) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> { error: <span class="tok-str">"Could not archive the program."</span> };

  <span class="tok-fn">revalidateTag</span>(<span class="tok-str">`program-${id}`</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> {};
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Same interaction through a shared endpoint. The endpoint's shape and error conventions serve
      every caller it has, so they can never be tailored to this one; a
      <code>404</code> is ambiguous in ways <code>findUnique</code> returning
      <code>null</code> never is, and type safety ends at the HTTP boundary.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Be precise about what kind of step down this is: for a single application, the centralized API
    is <strong>a step down on the platform side</strong>, not a wrong architecture. The HTTP
    boundary erases types, so the contract has to be rebuilt by hand: the platform now needs a <strong
      >standardized error and response protocol</strong
    > that every endpoint follows and every caller parses, purely so the calling logic can understand
    <em>exactly what happened</em> (not-found vs forbidden vs conflict vs validation) and respond appropriately.
    That protocol is real, ongoing engineering, and it is the thing <code>findUnique</code> returning
    a typed <code>null</code> gives you for free.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the centralized API has real merit where the organization calls for it: in polyglot repos,
    or wherever business logic must be shared across many products and runtimes, one backend serving
    them all is often the right call, and the protocol work is simply its price. Worth remembering
    even then: <strong>centralizing business logic does not require an HTTP wire.</strong> A shared server-side
    package imported by every app that needs it centralizes the code while keeping the types, and it's
    often the better first reach when all the consumers are TypeScript. So the gradient reads: the ORM
    inside the action is the optimum for an app that owns its data; the centralized API is more platform
    work, taken on for organizational reasons that are often good ones. The kicker is the <strong
      >proxy</strong
    > (your own API route forwarding to the real backend): the worst of both worlds. It pays the full
    protocol-and-types cost of the HTTP layer <em>and</em> adds a hop of latency and a second error surface,
    while providing nothing the direct call doesn't.
  </p>
  <p>
    Step back far enough and a deeper point comes into view. The HTTP API between a UI and its data
    is not just a cost to be managed; it is <strong
      >exactly the thing this architecture was built to eliminate</strong
    >. Server components and server actions exist to bring the data layer and the business logic to
    where they are used, directly at the UI layer: the component awaits the query, the action runs
    the mutation, and the rendered result is the contract (<a href='04-ssr.html#api-is-html'>§4.2</a
    >: the API is your HTML). <strong>The UI is your API.</strong> That reframes the centralization question
    too: if business logic must be shared across products, one way to share it is not a data API at all,
    but <em>microfrontends</em>: centrally owned, data-connected components that other apps compose
    in. What crosses that boundary is finished, typed UI rather than raw JSON, which makes it a
    dramatically more type-safe API surface than a data layer. The honest trade is flexibility: the
    moment a consumer is not React (a mobile app, a partner integration, another stack), the
    component stops being consumable and the data API earns its place back.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='voice' title='Fewer middlemen'>
    <p>
      The principle is blunt. Use server actions that talk to your real backend services directly,
      rather than proxying mutations through Next.js API routes on the same app. Every HTTP hop you
      remove is type safety you keep, latency you save, and a class of errors you no longer have to
      handle. And once the mutation lands, the parent refreshes through the cache, not a callback (<a
        href='#refresh'>§5.8</a
      >,
      <a href='15-dedupe-caching.html#server'>§15.2</a>), so the write stays as decoupled as the
      read.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='forms'>5.11 Forms and actions, end to end</h2>
  <p>
    If a server action is a typed remote procedure call, the natural way to invoke one is a form,
    and React 19 and Next.js have quietly rebuilt the entire form pipeline around that idea. The
    pieces are the <code>action</code> prop, <code>useActionState</code>,
    <code>useFormStatus</code>, and Next's <code>&lt;Form&gt;</code> component; each one deletes a piece
    of the form code that used to be written by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with the <code>action</code> prop.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> In React 19 a form's <code>action</code> can be a <em>function</em>, and
    when the user submits, React calls it with the form's <code>FormData</code>. That single change
    retires the whole <code>onSubmit</code> ritual: no <code>preventDefault()</code>, no reading
    fields into state, no hand-rolled <code>fetch</code>, no <code>isSubmitting</code> flag. And when
    the function is a <em>server action</em>, submitting the form <em>is</em> the RPC of
    <a href='#server-actions'>§5.10</a>: fields go in as arguments, the typed result comes back.
    Because the wiring is declarative, it also works before hydration: submit early and the browser
    posts natively, and Next replays the submission into the action, so a slow connection degrades
    to a normal form instead of a dead button.
  </p>
  <p>
    Next question: where does the action's <em>result</em> live? That is
    <code>useActionState</code>.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' /> It wraps the action and hands back three things: the last result, a bound action
    to give the form, and a pending flag. The action's return value (the
    <code>&#123; error &#125;</code> result shape of <a href='#self-contained'>§5.7</a>) simply
    <em>becomes state</em>, which is exactly where a validation message wants to be:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/programs/CreateProgramForm.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useActionState } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { createProgram } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/app/programs/actions"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">CreateProgramForm</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [result, formAction, isPending] = <span class="tok-fn">useActionState</span>(createProgram, <span class="tok-kw">null</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;form <span class="tok-prop">action</span>={formAction}&gt;
      &lt;input <span class="tok-prop">name</span>=<span class="tok-str">"name"</span> <span class="tok-prop">required</span> <span class="tok-prop">disabled</span>={isPending} /&gt;
      {result?.error &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">FieldError</span>&gt;{result.error}&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">FieldError</span>&gt;}
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SubmitButton</span>&gt;Create program&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">SubmitButton</span>&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The whole submit lifecycle in one hook: the action runs on the server, its return value lands
      in <code>result</code>, and the error renders inline on the next line. Count what is absent:
      no <code>onSubmit</code>, no <code>fetch</code>, no submitting flag, no state to reset.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The submit button is deliberately not written inline, because pending state is where forms
    usually grow wires. <code>useFormStatus</code>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' /> lets a leaf component read the status of the <em
      >form it happens to be inside</em
    >, with no props and no context of ours:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ui/SubmitButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useFormStatus } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react-dom"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SubmitButton</span>({ children }: { children: <span class="tok-fn">ReactNode</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-com">// The status of the nearest enclosing &lt;form&gt;, read like context.</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { pending } = <span class="tok-fn">useFormStatus</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span> <span class="tok-prop">type</span>=<span class="tok-str">"submit"</span> <span class="tok-prop">disabled</span>={pending} <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>={pending}&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Write this once in the ui package and every form in the app gets a correct submit button by
      composition: it disables and spins during any submission, with zero wiring from the form above
      it. This is <a href='14-minimize-the-wires.html'>§14</a>'s argument in form clothing. The one
      rule: it must render <em>inside</em> the form, because the hook reads the nearest enclosing one.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Finally, Next's <code>&lt;Form&gt;</code> component (<code>next/form</code>) extends the native
    element for the <em>other</em> kind of form: the one whose submit is a navigation, not a mutation.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> Give it a <strong>string</strong>
    <code>action</code> and the fields become query parameters of that path, navigated to client-side,
    with the target route prefetched while the user is still typing. Give it a <strong
      >function</strong
    > and it behaves like the mutation form above. The string form is <a
      href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a
    > arriving by another road: the form writes the URL, and the URL is the state.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/programs/ProgramSearch.tsx - a server component; no directive needed</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> Form <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/form"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramSearch</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Form</span> <span class="tok-prop">action</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/programs"</span>&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* Submit navigates to /programs?q=… client-side; the route is prefetched. */}</span>
      &lt;input <span class="tok-prop">name</span>=<span class="tok-str">"q"</span> <span class="tok-prop">placeholder</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Search programs"</span> /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SubmitButton</span>&gt;Search&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">SubmitButton</span>&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Form</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      A search form with no client code of its own: the shell is a server component, and the only
      JavaScript on the page is the shared submit button. Before hydration it still works as a plain
      GET form. The page it lands on reads <code>searchParams</code> on the server (<a
        href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a
      >), so the result is shareable, back-button-friendly, and server-rendered.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The decision rule that falls out: <strong
      >a submit that changes data is a function action through <code>useActionState</code>; a submit
      that changes what you're looking at is a string action writing the URL.</strong
    > Both get pending state from the same shared button, both work before hydration, and neither needs
    an API route, an <code>onSubmit</code>, or a byte of hand-rolled fetch code. The per-primitive
    reference entries (signatures, options, doc links) stay in <a href='19-new-primitives.html'
      >§19</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Components are server components
          <strong>until an interaction atom proves otherwise</strong>: push every boundary to the
          smallest leaf that contains the interaction.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>No <code>"use client"</code> in the app directory</strong>: the scaffold stays
          server; client code is imported into it. Named exceptions:
          <code>error.tsx</code>, genuinely single-surface interactive pages.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Interactive shells receive server content via <code>children</code>/slots;
          <strong>never import what they wrap</strong>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Third-party interactive components get
          <strong>a dedicated <code>"use client"</code> wrapper file</strong>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Data layer imports <code>server-only</code>;
          <strong>secrets never ride a boundary move</strong> into
          <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_*</code>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Providers are <strong
            >thin client wrappers around <code>&#123;children&#125;</code></strong
          >, close to the root, converting nothing.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Interactive leaves are
          <strong
            >self-contained: the button owns its click, pending state, action call, and any dialog
            or floating element it opens</strong
          >. The parent passes identity, nothing else.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          After a mutation, <strong>the server is the source of truth</strong>:
          <code>revalidateTag</code>/<code>revalidatePath</code> (or a keyed SWR
          <code>mutate</code> in client islands), never refetch callbacks threaded to leaves.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>No God components</strong>: nothing may fetch the data, own the UI state, and
          dispense the callbacks from the top of a tree.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <GoDeeper>
    <p>
      React 19 docs:
      <a href='https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
        >'use client'</a
      >
      ·
      <a href='https://react.dev/reference/rsc/server-components' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
        >Server Components</a
      >. Next.js docs:
      <a
        href='https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/server-and-client-components'
        target='_blank'
        rel='noopener'>Server and Client Components</a
      >
      (composition patterns, third-party wrappers, context providers). Full list in
      <a href='20-references.html'>§20</a>.
    </p>
  </GoDeeper>
